2026-06-30 14:57:15,880 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-61cb9739-bf7c-418f-b1da-3bd45d6334ee', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\n20170818_10mikro_eP_BS3.raw\n20170818_5mikro_eP_BS3.raw\n20171123_IG1_in_vivo_MS2.raw\n20171123_Ig2_in_vivo_MS2.raw\n20171123_Ig3_in_vivo_MS2.raw\n20171123_Ig4_in_vivo_MS2.raw\n20171123_hP1_in_vivo_MS2.raw\n20171123_hP2_in_vivo_MS2.raw\n20171123_hP3_in_vivo_MS2.raw\n20171123_hP4_in_vivo_MS2.raw\n20171215_Ig1_DMSO.raw\n20171215_Ig1_DSSO.raw\n20171215_Ig2_DMSO.raw\n20171215_Ig2_DSSO.raw\n20171215_Ig3_DMSO.raw\n20171215_Ig3_DSSO.raw\n20171215_Ig4_DMSO.raw\n20171215_Ig4_DSSO.raw\n20171215_eP1_DMSO.raw\n20171215_eP1_DSSO.raw\n20171215_eP2_DMSO.raw\n20171215_eP2_DSSO.raw\n20171215_eP3_DMSO.raw\n20171215_eP3_DSSO.raw\n20171215_eP4_DMSO.raw\n20171215_eP4_DSSO.raw\n20171220_1_1_hXP_EThcD.raw\n20171220_1_1_hXP_MS3.raw\n20171220_2_1_hXP_EThcD.raw\n20171220_2_1_hXP_MS3.raw\n20171220_3_1_hXP_EThcD.raw\n20171220_3_1_hXP_MS3.raw\nCoIP_Ig_DMSO_1_MS2.raw\nCoIP_Ig_DMSO_2_MS2.raw\nCoIP_Ig_DMSO_3_MS2.raw\nCoIP_hP_DMSO_1_MS2.raw\nCoIP_hP_DMSO_2_MS2.raw\nCoIP_hP_DMSO_3_MS2.raw\nDeltaHP_1zu1000_SCX_DSSO_EThcD.raw\nDeltaHP_1zu1000_SCX_DSSO_MS3_HCD.raw\nListeria_XP_Lauf1_2.raw\nListeria_XP_Lauf1_2_171013045044.raw\nListeria_XP_Lauf2_1.raw\nListeria_XP_Lauf2_1_171013185049.raw\nListeria_XP_Lauf3_2.raw\nListeria_XP_Lauf3_2_171013141051.raw\nP1P2_1zu1000_SCX_DSSO_MS3_EThcD.raw\nP1P2_1zu1000_SCX_DSSO_MS3_HCD.raw\nP1P2_SCX_1zu300_DSSO_MS3_EThcD.raw\nP1P2_SCX_1zu300_DSSO_MS3_HCD.raw\nP1P2_SCX_1zu500_DSSO_MS3_EThcD.raw\nP1P2_SCX_1zu500_DSSO_MS3_HCD.raw\nXP_4_5_lowflow_SCX2h_DSSO_EThcD.raw\nXP_4_5_lowflow_SCX2h_DSSO_MS3_HCD.raw\nXP_6_25_lowflow_SCX2h_DSSO_MS3_EThcD.raw\nXP_6_25_lowflow_SCX2h_DSSO_MS3_HCD.raw\naP_1zu1000_SCX_DSSO_EThcD.raw\naP_1zu1000_SCX_DSSO_MS3_HCD.raw\naP_1zu300_SCX1M_DSSO_EThcD.raw\naP_1zu300_SCX1M_DSSO_MS3_HCD.raw\naP_1zu500_SCX1M_DSSO_MS3_EThcD.raw\naP_1zu500_SCX1M_DSSO_MS3_HCD.raw\nhP_SCX_1zu300_DSSO_MS3_EThcD.raw\nhP_SCX_1zu300_DSSO_MS3_HCD.raw\nhP_SCX_1zu500_DSSO_MS3_EThcD.raw\nhP_SCX_1zu500_DSSO_MS3_HCD.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nChemical cross-linking enables drafting ClpXP proximity maps and taking snapshots of in vivo interaction networks\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nProtein-protein interactions within complexes and networks are often dynamic and their elucidation remains a challenging task. Here, we show on the example of the proteolytic ClpXP complex the power of combined chemical cross-linking and mass-spectrometry to capture transient binding interactions within ClpP and ClpX as well as across the enigmatic ClpX hexamer – ClpP heptamer interface. Our data suggests that a few hot spot lysine residues located in signature loops in ClpX mediate the ClpX-ClpP interaction. This study further confirms that Listeria monocytogenes ClpX solely interacts with the heterooligomeric ClpP1/2 complex via the ClpP2 apical site. Moreover, the cellular interaction network of human and bacterial proteases was elucidated via in situ chemical cross-linking followed by an antibody-based pull-down against ClpP from genetically unmodified cells. A subsequent gel-free, quantitative mass spectrometric analysis demonstrated an up to 3-fold higher coverage compared to conventional co-immunoprecipitation without cross-linker revealing unprecedented insight into intracellular ClpXP networks.\n\n[METHODS]\nRecombinant ClpP and ClpXP were expressed in E. coli BL21 (DE3) cells and cross-linked with the MS-cleavable cross-linker DSSO. Proteins were denatured, reduced, alkylated and digested in solution using trypsin. Afterwards cross-linked peptides were enriched via cation exchange and measured on a UltiMate 3000 nano HPLC system (Thermo Fischer Scientific Inc., Waltham, Massachusetts, USA) equipped with an Acclaim C18 PepMap100 75 µm ID x 2 cm trap and an Acclaim PepMap RSLC C18 separation column (75 µm ID x 50 cm) coupled to an EASY-source equipped Thermo Fischer LTQ Orbitrap Fusion mass spectrometer (Thermo Fisher Scientific Inc., Waltham, Massachusetts, USA). Known EThcD and MS3 fragmentation strategies for DSSO cross-linked peptides were used for MS analysis. Human liver derived cell line HepG2 and E. coli K12 cells were cross-linked in situ with DSSO. Cells were lysed and protein amount was adjusted for subsequent anti-ClpP pull-down. Proteins were denatured, reduced, alkylated and digested in solution using trypsin. Afterwards samples were measured on a UltiMate 3000 nano HPLC system (Thermo Fischer Scientific Inc., Waltham, Massachusetts, USA) equipped with an Acclaim C18 PepMap100 75 µm ID x 2 cm trap and an Acclaim PepMap RSLC C18 separation column (75 µm ID x 50 cm) coupled to an EASY-source equipped Thermo Fischer LTQ Orbitrap Fusion mass spectrometer (Thermo Fisher Scientific Inc., Waltham, Massachusetts, USA). During sample separation MS full scans were performed at 120,000 resolution in the orbitrap with quadrupole isolation. The MS instrument was operated in a 3 s top speed data dependent mode. The scan range was set from 300 to 1,500 m/z with 60% RF lens amplitude. The AGC target was set to 2.0e5, the maximum ion injection time was 50 ms and internal calibration of the EASY source was set on. Peptides with intensity higher than 5.0e3 and charge state 2-7 were selected for MS2 scans, performed in the ion trap. The isolation window was set to 1.6 m/z with 120 m/z as first mass. Peptides were fragmented with HCD (30%) and ion trap operating in rapid mode. The AGC target was set to 1.0e4 with maximum injection time of 100 ms and ions were injected for all available parallelizable time. Dynamic exclusion time was 60 s with 10 ppm low and high mass tolerance.\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nEscherichia coli; Homo sapiens (human); Staphylococcus aureus\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
