2026-06-30 14:57:51,387 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-175edf83-f6f1-4485-b52f-eae15fc0ce2d', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms from the provided publication text and/or PRIDE project description.\nFor each term, return the best available ontology-resolved value with its accession where indicated.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: organism\nsdrf_column: characteristics[organism]\nontology: NCBITaxon (e.g. NCBITaxon:9606)\ndescription: Source species of the proteome of interest in the biological samples analyzed by mass spectrometry. Return the scientific name. \nSource species maybe different than host species when proteins from one organism are expressed in another. \ninstructions: Use NCBITaxon accession. Common examples: Homo sapiens (NCBITaxon:9606),\n  Mus musculus (NCBITaxon:10090), Saccharomyces cerevisiae (NCBITaxon:4932),\n  Escherichia coli (NCBITaxon:562). If multiple species, return a list.\n\nterm: organism part\nsdrf_column: characteristics[organism part]\nontology: UBERON or BTO\ndescription: Main normalized anatomical term for the sample analyzed by mass spectrometry. \ninstructions: Use the clearest ontology-backed anatomy term. Examples: whole-cell lysate, mitochondria,\n  nucleus, cytoplasm, liver, heart, brain, cell, ect. Return null if not applicable\n  (e.g. purified recombinant proteins with no tissue origin).\n\nterm: cell line\nsdrf_column: characteristics[cell line]\nontology: CLO, BTO, or EFO (e.g. CLO:0001230 for HEK293)\ndescription: Name of the cell line if a cell line was used.\ninstructions: Return null if no cell line was used (primary cells or tissues).\n\nterm: cell type\nsdrf_column: characteristics[cell type]\nontology: CL, BTO, or CLO\ndescription: Cell type of the sample.\ninstructions: Return null if not applicable or not stated.\n\nterm: material type\nsdrf_column: characteristics[material type]\nontology: PRIDE:0000837\ndescription: Type of biological material analyzed. Differentiates whether the whole cell lysate was analyzed, just a specific anatomical structure or tissue, or if the samples were purified for specific proteins before mass spectrometry analysis. \nallowed_values: tissue, whole cell lysate, organell, membrane, synthetic, purified proteins, affinity captured\ninstructions: Choose the best matching fixed value. Return null if unclear.\n\nterm: strain or breed\nsdrf_column: characteristics[strain or breed]\nontology: free text\ndescription: Strain, breed, cultivar, or ecotype of the organism.\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: developmental stage\nsdrf_column: characteristics[developmental stage]\nontology: EFO\ndescription: Developmental stage of the organism or donor.\ninstructions: Return null if not stated or not applicable.\n\nterm: technology type\nsdrf_column: technology type\nontology: PRIDE:0000663\ndescription: Type of technology used. For XL-MS this is always fixed.\nallowed_values: proteomic profiling by mass spectrometry\ninstructions: Always return "proteomic profiling by mass spectrometry" for XL-MS datasets.\n\nterm: source name\nsdrf_column: source name\nontology: PRIDE:0000623\ndescription: Unique identifier for the biological sample. Typically derived from\n  the dataset file names or sample descriptions in the paper.\ninstructions: Describe the naming convention used (e.g. "PXD_stem" or "condition_replicate").\n  This is auto-generated from file names; return a description of the sample naming pattern only.\n\nterm: assay name\nsdrf_column: assay name\nontology: NCIT:C178857\ndescription: Unique identifier for each MS run/assay. Typically the raw file stem.\ninstructions: Describe the naming convention. This is auto-generated; return the pattern only.\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable (e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"), else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text supporting the extraction, or null\n  "evidence_location": where in the paper (e.g. "Methods", "Abstract"), or null\n\nExample output structure:\n{\n  "characteristics[organism]": {"value": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae", "accession": "NCBITaxon:4932", "confidence": "high", "evidence_quote": "...", "evidence_location": "Methods"},\n  "characteristics[organism part]": {"value": "mitochondria", "accession": null, "confidence": "high", "evidence_quote": "...", "evidence_location": "Methods"},\n  ...\n}'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nChemical cross-linking enables drafting ClpXP proximity maps and taking snapshots of in vivo interaction networks\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nProtein-protein interactions within complexes and networks are often dynamic and their elucidation remains a challenging task. Here, we show on the example of the proteolytic ClpXP complex the power of combined chemical cross-linking and mass-spectrometry to capture transient binding interactions within ClpP and ClpX as well as across the enigmatic ClpX hexamer – ClpP heptamer interface. Our data suggests that a few hot spot lysine residues located in signature loops in ClpX mediate the ClpX-ClpP interaction. This study further confirms that Listeria monocytogenes ClpX solely interacts with the heterooligomeric ClpP1/2 complex via the ClpP2 apical site. Moreover, the cellular interaction network of human and bacterial proteases was elucidated via in situ chemical cross-linking followed by an antibody-based pull-down against ClpP from genetically unmodified cells. A subsequent gel-free, quantitative mass spectrometric analysis demonstrated an up to 3-fold higher coverage compared to conventional co-immunoprecipitation without cross-linker revealing unprecedented insight into intracellular ClpXP networks.\n\n[METHODS]\nRecombinant ClpP and ClpXP were expressed in E. coli BL21 (DE3) cells and cross-linked with the MS-cleavable cross-linker DSSO. Proteins were denatured, reduced, alkylated and digested in solution using trypsin. Afterwards cross-linked peptides were enriched via cation exchange and measured on a UltiMate 3000 nano HPLC system (Thermo Fischer Scientific Inc., Waltham, Massachusetts, USA) equipped with an Acclaim C18 PepMap100 75 µm ID x 2 cm trap and an Acclaim PepMap RSLC C18 separation column (75 µm ID x 50 cm) coupled to an EASY-source equipped Thermo Fischer LTQ Orbitrap Fusion mass spectrometer (Thermo Fisher Scientific Inc., Waltham, Massachusetts, USA). Known EThcD and MS3 fragmentation strategies for DSSO cross-linked peptides were used for MS analysis. Human liver derived cell line HepG2 and E. coli K12 cells were cross-linked in situ with DSSO. Cells were lysed and protein amount was adjusted for subsequent anti-ClpP pull-down. Proteins were denatured, reduced, alkylated and digested in solution using trypsin. Afterwards samples were measured on a UltiMate 3000 nano HPLC system (Thermo Fischer Scientific Inc., Waltham, Massachusetts, USA) equipped with an Acclaim C18 PepMap100 75 µm ID x 2 cm trap and an Acclaim PepMap RSLC C18 separation column (75 µm ID x 50 cm) coupled to an EASY-source equipped Thermo Fischer LTQ Orbitrap Fusion mass spectrometer (Thermo Fisher Scientific Inc., Waltham, Massachusetts, USA). During sample separation MS full scans were performed at 120,000 resolution in the orbitrap with quadrupole isolation. The MS instrument was operated in a 3 s top speed data dependent mode. The scan range was set from 300 to 1,500 m/z with 60% RF lens amplitude. The AGC target was set to 2.0e5, the maximum ion injection time was 50 ms and internal calibration of the EASY source was set on. Peptides with intensity higher than 5.0e3 and charge state 2-7 were selected for MS2 scans, performed in the ion trap. The isolation window was set to 1.6 m/z with 120 m/z as first mass. Peptides were fragmented with HCD (30%) and ion trap operating in rapid mode. The AGC target was set to 1.0e4 with maximum injection time of 100 ms and ions were injected for all available parallelizable time. Dynamic exclusion time was 60 s with 10 ppm low and high mass tolerance.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
